  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component is fundamentally inaccessible to keyboard and assistive technology users. It uses a `<div>` for an interactive trigger without keyboard handlers or ARIA attributes, completely violating the WAI-ARIA Disclosure pattern. Screen reader users receive no indication of interactivity or state, and keyboard users cannot reach or activate the control.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\nBefore reading the code, I predicted:\n1. Semantic HTML violation: `<div>` used as a button instead of `<button>`.\n2. Keyboard inaccessibility: No `tabIndex`, no `onKeyDown` handler for Enter/Space.\n3. Missing ARIA state: No `aria-expanded` to communicate toggle state to AT.\n4. Redundant text symbols: Visual `+`/`\u2212` will be announced redundantly if ARIA is added later without `aria-hidden`.\n\nAll predictions were confirmed during investigation.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Semantic HTML & Keyboard Activation Missing**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:10-13` where the trigger is a `<div className=\"expandable-trigger\">` with only an `onClick` handler.\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users, Screen reader users.\n   - **Expected Behavior**: Per WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard) and WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern, interactive controls must be native HTML elements (`<button>`) or have `tabIndex={0}` with explicit Enter/Space keyboard handlers.\n   - **Fix**: Replace `<div>` with `<button type=\"button\" className=\"expandable-trigger\">`. Native buttons are focusable by default and activate on Enter/Space, eliminating the need for custom keyboard event listeners.\n\n2. **Focus Management & Tab Order**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:10` (`<div>` is not in the DOM tab order).\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users.\n   - **Expected Behavior**: Per WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order), interactive elements must be reachable via Tab and follow a logical sequence.\n   - **Fix**: Resolved by switching to `<button>`. If a `<div>` must remain for framework constraints, add `tabIndex={0}` and an `onKeyDown` handler checking for `e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' '`.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Missing ARIA State Communication (`aria-expanded`)**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:8-9` where `isExpanded` state is used only for conditional rendering, not exposed to the DOM.\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users.\n   - **Expected Behavior**: Per WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value) and WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern, toggle controls must expose their state via `aria-expanded=\"true\"` or `\"false\"`.\n   - **Fix**: Add `aria-expanded={isExpanded}` to the trigger element.\n\n2. **Missing ARIA Association (`aria-controls`)**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:18-21` where content is conditionally rendered but has no ID, and the trigger has no reference to it.\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users.\n   - **Expected Behavior**: Per WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) and WAI-ARIA APG Disclosure pattern, the trigger must reference the controlled region via `aria-controls`, and the region must have a matching `id`.\n   - **Fix**: \n     ```jsx\n     // Trigger: aria-controls=\"expandable-content\"\n     // Content: <div id=\"expandable-content\" className=\"expandable-content\">\n     ```\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n1. **Redundant Visual Symbols Announced to AT**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyExpandable.jsx:14-15` where `{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}` is rendered as text.\n   - **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users.\n   - **Expected Behavior**: Per WCAG 1.3.1 and 4.1.2, visual state indicators that duplicate programmatic ARIA states should be hidden from assistive technology to prevent redundant/confusing announcements (e.g., \"Title, button, expanded, minus\").\n   - **Fix**: Wrap symbols in `<span aria-hidden=\"true\" className=\"expandable-icon\">{isExpanded ? ' \u2212' : ' +'}</span>`.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- **Missing `aria-describedby` or `aria-label` context**: If the title alone isn't descriptive enough for the expanded content, the trigger needs a more specific accessible name.\n- **Missing focus management on open/close**: When the section expands, focus does not move. While keeping focus on the trigger is often acceptable for disclosure widgets, it should be explicitly documented or tested to ensure it doesn't confuse users expecting focus to follow the revealed content.\n- **Missing `aria-hidden` on collapsed content**: When `isExpanded` is false, the content is unmounted (`{isExpanded && ...}`). If this were conditionally hidden via CSS instead of unmounting, it would need `aria-hidden={true}` to prevent AT from reading hidden content.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n- **Screen reader user**: Hears only the title text. No indication it's a button, no announcement of expandable state, and no link to the hidden content. The component is invisible as an interactive widget.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Cannot Tab to the trigger. Cannot activate it with Enter or Space. The feature is completely unusable.\n- **Low vision user (200% zoom, high contrast)**: If styled to look like a button, visual affordance contradicts keyboard reality. Focus indicator will never appear since the element isn't focusable.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: The `+`/`\u2212` symbol is a standard cognitive affordance for expand/collapse, but without keyboard focus or AT announcement, it relies entirely on sighted users to understand its purpose.\n\n**Verdict Justification**: \nThe component fails at the foundational level of web accessibility: semantic HTML and keyboard operability. Using a `<div>` for an interactive toggle excludes keyboard users entirely and provides zero programmatic context to assistive technology. The missing `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` break the WAI-ARIA Disclosure pattern completely. This requires immediate structural remediation before any visual styling or state logic is considered.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the parent application rely on this component being unmountable for performance reasons? If so, we may need to keep the `<div>` but must implement full keyboard handlers and ARIA attributes, though `<button>` with conditional rendering is still strongly preferred.\n- Are there multiple instances of this component on a page? If so, `id` values for `aria-controls` must be unique per instance (e.g., using a generated ID or index).",
